* counter&(1<<j)

1<<j will create a binary output whose jth bit is set:
1 = 000000001
if j = 6
1<<j = 001000000
if counter = 320 = 101000000
counter&(1<<j) = 101000000 & 001000000 = 001000000 (only get the 1 in that perticular place in which both of
them have got 1)

This technique can help us to find if the jth element in counter is set or not(meaning it is 1 or not)
We can also do this with bitmasking: 320&(1<<6) = 64

Number of digits in binary form of positive integer n: (int)log2(n) + 1
Number of digits in decimal form: floor(log10(n))+1